export const pkUnit03CommonInfoMarkdown: LocalizedText = {
  "en": "# PK Unit 3: Colours and Numbers — Common Info\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview\n\nPK Unit 3: Colours and Numbers helps children use early colour and number language in meaningful play, classroom routines, art, music, movement, construction, sorting, and simple problem-solving. Compared with PG, PK children can work with a broader colour set, more stable one-to-one counting, simple quantity choices, and short response chunks when ready.\n\nThis unit should still remain concrete and activity-based. Colours and numbers are not treated as worksheet targets or abstract recall drills. Children learn through touching, choosing, matching, sorting, counting objects, giving quantities, building with coloured materials, moving to number cues, and participating in teacher-supported games.\n\nThe core PK colour focus includes `red ｜ blue ｜ yellow ｜ green`, with optional exposure to `orange ｜ purple ｜ pink`. The core number focus includes `one ｜ two ｜ three ｜ four ｜ five`, with optional exposure to `six` through songs or routines. PK children may attempt colour or number words, but full question-answer accuracy is not required as the baseline.\n\n## 2. Unit Language\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary\n\n```text\nred ｜ blue ｜ yellow ｜ green ｜ colour ｜ one ｜ two ｜ three ｜ four ｜ five ｜ number ｜ same ｜ different ｜ big ｜ small ｜ circle ｜ star ｜ block ｜ ball ｜ crayon ｜ sticker\n```\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks\n\n```text\nWhat colour? ｜ Red or blue? ｜ Show me red. ｜ Point to green. ｜ Find yellow. ｜ Same colour. ｜ Different colour. ｜ Give me one. ｜ Give me two. ｜ Give me three. ｜ Count with me. ｜ One, two, three. ｜ How many? ｜ Big or small? ｜ Put it here. ｜ Match the colour. ｜ Sort the colours. ｜ Clean up.\n```\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes\n\n### Week 1: Red, Blue, Yellow and Green\n\nChildren explore core colours through real objects, blocks, balls, scarves, crayons, stickers, paint, and classroom hunts. The focus is recognition, choice, matching, and optional colour-word attempts.\n\n### Week 2: One to Five\n\nChildren experience numbers through counting objects, clapping, tapping, jumping, building towers, giving quantities, and simple group routines. The focus is concrete quantity and supported counting.\n\n### Week 3: Matching, Sorting and Same/Different\n\nChildren use colour and number knowledge to match, sort, group, compare same/different, and organise materials into baskets, trays, hoops, or construction spaces.\n\n### Week 4: Colour and Number Play Projects\n\nChildren integrate colour and number in art, music, movement, construction, pretend shops, classroom displays, and clean-up routines: three red blocks, two green stickers, five claps, colour paths, number towers, and shared group projects.\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool\n\n### Colour Input\n\nred ｜ blue ｜ yellow ｜ green ｜ colour ｜ same colour ｜ different colour ｜ red one ｜ blue one ｜ green one ｜ show me red ｜ find green ｜ red or blue?\n\n### Number Input\n\none ｜ two ｜ three ｜ four ｜ five ｜ number ｜ count ｜ how many ｜ one more ｜ give me two ｜ give me three ｜ count with me ｜ one, two, three, four, five\n\n### Shape and Object Input\n\ncircle ｜ star ｜ block ｜ ball ｜ crayon ｜ sticker ｜ basket ｜ tray ｜ hoop ｜ tower ｜ big ｜ small\n\n### Teacher Routine Language\n\nLook. ｜ Show me red. ｜ Point to green. ｜ Find yellow. ｜ Red or blue? ｜ Same colour. ｜ Different colour. ｜ Count with me. ｜ Give me three. ｜ How many? ｜ Put it here. ｜ Sort the colours. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Good job. ｜ All done.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes\n\nChildren may respond by looking, pointing, choosing between colour or number options, naming a colour or number when ready, matching same-colour objects, sorting by colour, giving one to five objects with support, counting along with teacher, showing fingers, clapping or jumping to a count, building number towers, placing stickers or blocks by quantity, and cleaning up materials by colour or group.\n\nChildren are not expected to answer all `What colour?` or `How many?` questions accurately, count independently beyond five, recognise written numerals as a required outcome, write numbers, complete worksheet tasks, or explain mathematical concepts verbally.\n\n## 6. Theme Songs / Chants\n\n### Core Songs\n\nI See Something Blue  \nI See Something Pink  \nWhat's Your Favorite Color  \nRed Yellow Green Blue  \nOne Potato, Two Potatoes  \nCount & Move  \nHow Many Fingers  \n10 Little Buses  \nFive Little Ducks  \nFive Little Monkeys  \nPop The Bubbles\n\n### Use of Songs\n\nSongs should support colour noticing, object search, counting actions, finger play, jumping, clapping, moving, sorting, and group routines. Children may sing repeated colour or number words when ready, but pointing, moving, clapping, tapping, showing fingers, and choosing objects remain valid participation.\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces\n\nClassroom  \nArt classroom  \nLego area  \nConstruction / block room  \nMusic classroom  \nIndoor physical fitness area  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nScience classroom  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nTable activity area\n\n### Useful Materials\n\nRed / blue / yellow / green objects  \nColour cards  \nColoured blocks  \nLego / Duplo bricks  \nBalls  \nBeanbags  \nScarves  \nStickers  \nCrayons  \nPaint  \nSorting trays  \nBaskets  \nHoops  \nNumber dot cards  \nFinger-play props  \nLarge paper  \nPom-poms  \nNatural materials  \nShape cards  \nNumber towers  \nVisual clean-up cards\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities\n\nThe Lego area can support colour sorting, tower counting, and same/different grouping. The art classroom can support colour mixing, printing, stamping, sticker counting, and shared displays. Movement spaces can support count-and-move routines. Science materials can support safe colour observation and mixing, but the unit should not become a formal science lesson.\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes\n\nThis unit should not become a worksheet-based maths or colour-testing unit.\n\nThis unit may use `What colour?` and `How many?` as teacher input, but children do not need to answer accurately every time.\n\nThis unit should not require written numeral recognition or number writing.\n\nThis unit should keep quantity work concrete and small. Five is the stable PK target; six may appear incidentally through songs or materials.\n\nThis unit should maintain play, movement, sensory exploration, and routine use as the main learning pathway.\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus\n\nTeachers observe whether children recognise and choose core colours, attempt colour words, match and sort same-colour materials, participate in one-to-five counting routines, give small quantities with support, show fingers or count along, build and compare small groups, follow colour/number clean-up routines, and engage with colour-number play confidently.\n\nObservation should focus on concrete recognition, matching, sorting, counting participation, quantity awareness, turn-taking, material use, and readiness-based short output rather than written or abstract mathematical performance.\n",
  "zh": "# PK Unit 3: Colours and Numbers — Common Info｜PK 单元 3：颜色与数字 — 通用信息\n\n## 1. Unit Theme Overview｜单元主题概览\n\nPK 单元 3：颜色与数字帮助儿童在有意义的游戏、课堂常规、艺术、音乐、动作、建构、分类和简单问题解决中使用早期的颜色和数字语言。和 PG 相比，PK 儿童可以使用更广泛的颜色集、更稳定的一一对应数数、简单的数量选择，以及准备好时的简短反应句块。\n\n本单元仍应保持具体和基于活动。颜色和数字不被视为练习册目标或抽象记忆训练。儿童通过触摸、选择、匹配、分类、数物品、给出数量、搭建有色材料、按数字提示移动，以及参与教师支持的游戏来学习。\n\n核心 PK 颜色聚焦包括 `red ｜ blue ｜ yellow ｜ green`，可选接触 `orange ｜ purple ｜ pink`。核心数字聚焦包括 `one ｜ two ｜ three ｜ four ｜ five`，可选通过歌曲或常规接触 `six`。PK 儿童可以尝试颜色或数字词，但不要求问答准确性作为基线。\n\n## 2. Unit Language｜单元语言\n\n### PK Core Vocabulary｜PK 核心词汇\n\nred ｜ blue ｜ yellow ｜ green ｜ colour ｜ one ｜ two ｜ three ｜ four ｜ five ｜ number ｜ same ｜ different ｜ big ｜ small ｜ circle ｜ star ｜ block ｜ ball ｜ crayon ｜ sticker\n\n### Functional Teacher Chunks｜实用教师句块\n\nWhat colour? ｜ Red or blue? ｜ Show me red. ｜ Point to green. ｜ Find yellow. ｜ Same colour. ｜ Different colour. ｜ Give me one. ｜ Give me two. ｜ Give me three. ｜ Count with me. ｜ One, two, three. ｜ How many? ｜ Big or small? ｜ Put it here. ｜ Match the colour. ｜ Sort the colours. ｜ Clean up.\n\n## 3. Weekly Subthemes｜周次子主题\n\n### Week 1: Red, Blue, Yellow and Green｜第一周：红、蓝、黄和绿\n\n儿童通过真实物品、积木、球、丝巾、蜡笔、贴纸、颜料和课堂寻宝来探索核心颜色。重点是识别、选择、匹配和可选的颜色词尝试。\n\n### Week 2: One to Five｜第二周：一到五\n\n儿童通过数物品、拍手、踏步、跳跃、搭塔、给出数量和简单的集体常规来体验数字。重点是具体数量和支持的数数。\n\n### Week 3: Matching, Sorting and Same/Different｜第三周：匹配、分类和相同／不同\n\n儿童使用颜色和数字知识来匹配、分类、分组、比较相同／不同，以及把材料组织到篮子、托盘、呼啦圈或建构空间中。\n\n### Week 4: Colour and Number Play Projects｜第四周：颜色与数字游戏项目\n\n儿童在艺术、音乐、动作、建构、假装商店、课堂展示和收拾常规中整合颜色和数字：三个红色积木、两个绿色贴纸、五次拍手、颜色路径、数字塔和共享的集体项目。\n\n## 4. Teacher Input Pool｜教师输入库\n\n### Colour Input｜颜色输入\n\nred ｜ blue ｜ yellow ｜ green ｜ colour ｜ same colour ｜ different colour ｜ red one ｜ blue one ｜ green one ｜ show me red ｜ find green ｜ red or blue?\n\n### Number Input｜数字输入\n\none ｜ two ｜ three ｜ four ｜ five ｜ number ｜ count ｜ how many ｜ one more ｜ give me two ｜ give me three ｜ count with me ｜ one, two, three, four, five\n\n### Shape and Object Input｜形状与物品输入\n\ncircle ｜ star ｜ block ｜ ball ｜ crayon ｜ sticker ｜ basket ｜ tray ｜ hoop ｜ tower ｜ big ｜ small\n\n### Teacher Routine Language｜教师常规语言\n\nLook. ｜ Show me red. ｜ Point to green. ｜ Find yellow. ｜ Red or blue? ｜ Same colour. ｜ Different colour. ｜ Count with me. ｜ Give me three. ｜ How many? ｜ Put it here. ｜ Sort the colours. ｜ Clean up. ｜ Good job. ｜ All done.\n\n## 5. Expected PK Response Modes｜预期 PK 反应方式\n\n儿童可能通过以下方式反应：看、指、在颜色或数字选项间选择、在准备好时命名一个颜色或数字、匹配同色物品、按颜色分类、在支持下给出一到五个物品、跟着教师数、举起手指、按数拍手或跳跃、搭建数字塔、按数量放置贴纸或积木，以及按颜色或组别收拾材料。\n\n不要求儿童每次都准确回答所有\"What colour?\"或\"How many?\"问题、独立数到五以上、把识别书面数字作为必需结果、写数字、完成练习册任务，或口头解释数学概念。\n\n## 6. Core Unit Songs｜单元核心歌曲\n\n### Core Songs｜核心歌曲\n\nI See Something Blue  \nI See Something Pink  \nWhat's Your Favorite Color  \nRed Yellow Green Blue  \nOne Potato, Two Potatoes  \nCount & Move  \nHow Many Fingers  \n10 Little Buses  \nFive Little Ducks  \nFive Little Monkeys  \nPop The Bubbles\n\n### Use of Songs｜歌曲的使用\n\n歌曲应当支持颜色留意、物品搜寻、数数动作、手指游戏、跳跃、拍手、移动、分类和集体常规。儿童在准备好时可以唱重复的颜色或数字词，但指、移动、拍手、踏步、举手和选择物品仍是有效的参与。\n\n## 7. Resource Opportunities｜资源机会\n\n### Useful EASTIE Spaces｜可用 EASTIE 空间\n\nClassroom  \nArt classroom  \nLego area  \nConstruction / block room  \nMusic classroom  \nIndoor physical fitness area  \nSmall outdoor grass area attached to PK classroom  \nScience classroom  \nCircle-time area / mat area  \nTable activity area\n\n### Useful Materials｜可用材料\n\nRed / blue / yellow / green objects  \nColour cards  \nColoured blocks  \nLego / Duplo bricks  \nBalls  \nBeanbags  \nScarves  \nStickers  \nCrayons  \nPaint  \nSorting trays  \nBaskets  \nHoops  \nNumber dot cards  \nFinger-play props  \nLarge paper  \nPom-poms  \nNatural materials  \nShape cards  \nNumber towers  \nVisual clean-up cards\n\n### Enhanced Resource Opportunities｜拓展资源机会\n\nLego 区可以支持颜色分类、搭塔数数和相同／不同分组。艺术教室可以支持混色、印刷、盖章、贴纸数数和共享展示。动作空间可以支持数数并移动的常规。科学材料可以支持安全的颜色观察和混色，但本单元不应变成正式科学课。\n\n## 8. Boundary Notes｜边界说明\n\n本单元不应变成基于练习册的数学或颜色测试单元。\n\n本单元可以把\"What colour?\"和\"How many?\"作为教师输入使用，但儿童不需要每次都准确回答。\n\n本单元不应要求书面数字识别或数字书写。\n\n本单元应把数量工作保持具体和小。五是稳定的 PK 目标；六可以偶然通过歌曲或材料出现。\n\n本单元应把游戏、动作、感官探索和常规使用作为主要学习路径。\n\n## 9. Unit-Level Observation Focus｜单元观察重点\n\n教师观察儿童是否识别并选择核心颜色、尝试颜色词、匹配和分类同色材料、参与一到五的数数常规、在支持下给出小数量、举手或跟着数、搭建和比较小数组、跟随颜色／数字收拾常规，以及自信地参与颜色—数字游戏。\n\n观察应聚焦于具体识别、匹配、分类、数数参与、数量意识、轮流、物品使用和准备度导向的简短输出，而非书面或抽象的数学表现。"
};

